</style><style>.lead-art img{aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:contain;background:#f7f7f7}.thumb img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}.thumb figcaption{font-size:10px;color:var(--ds-ink-2);padding-top:6px}.source-index a{overflow-wrap:anywhere}</style><style>.category-heading{margin:36px 0 18px;padding-top:18px;border-top:4px solid var(--ds-ink)}.story{padding:18px 0 22px;border-top:1px solid var(--ds-hairline)}.lead-art img{aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;background:var(--ds-panel)}.source-index li{padding:5px 0}.reading{overflow-wrap:anywhere}</style><meta content="The October 29 Edit" property="og:title"/><meta content="22 stories: new AI labs, open models, voice controls, infrastructure and space." property="og:description"/><meta content="https://hjbarraza.github.io/october-1-edit/2026-10-29/1-artemis29.jpg" property="og:image"/><meta content="https://hjbarraza.github.io/october-1-edit/2026-10-29/" property="og:url"/></head><body id="top"><a class="skip-link" href="#stories">Skip to the stories</a><main class="magazine"><div class="dateline"><span>29 OCTOBER 2026 · THURSDAY</span><span>22 STORIES · 1 EDITION</span></div><header><div class="header-tools"><span>THE THURSDAY EDIT · WEEKDAY EDITION</span><button aria-label="Switch to dark theme" aria-pressed="false" id="theme-toggle" type="button">Dark mode <span aria-hidden="true">◐</span></button></div><h1>The October 29 Edit</h1><p>22 stories. New model labs, inspectable voice AI, and the machinery behind the buildout.</p></header><div class="index-bar"><span>AI · Tools · Tech · Space · Business</span><nav><a href="#listen">Listen</a><a href="../#archive">Archive</a></nav></div><div class="listen listen-issue" id="listen"><div class="listen-heading"><span>LISTEN TO THE ISSUE</span><span>8:49 · SADACHBIA · 1×</span></div><div class="audio-part"><label for="daily-audio">Thursday briefing</label><audio id="daily-audio" preload="metadata" src="3-edit-2026-10-29.mp3">Your browser cannot play this audio. <a href="3-edit-2026-10-29.mp3">Download the recording</a></audio><div class="audio-player" data-audio="daily-audio" data-title="the Thursday briefing"><button aria-controls="daily-audio" aria-label="Play the Thursday briefing" class="audio-play" type="button"><span aria-hidden="true" class="play-symbol">▶</span></button><div class="audio-track"><div class="audio-status">Ready to listen</div><input aria-label="Seek in the Thursday briefing" aria-valuetext="0:00" class="audio-seek" max="100" min="0" step="0.1" type="range" value="0"/></div><span class="audio-time"><span class="elapsed">0:00</span> / <span class="duration">0:00</span></span></div><p class="audio-error" hidden="" role="status"></p><a class="audio-download" download="" href="3-edit-2026-10-29.mp3">Download audio ↓</a><noscript><p>Use the download link to listen. Interactive playback needs JavaScript.</p></noscript></div></div>
<p class="caption">Morning audio covers all 22 stories. Story numbers match the source index. Daily snapshot, not a live feed.</p><section class="edition" id="stories"><div class="edition-top"><span class="edition-kicker">THE ISSUE</span><span>22 stories</span></div><h2>Building the next layer</h2><figure class="lead-art"><img alt="The orange Artemis III core stage integrated between two solid rocket boosters inside Kennedy’s Vehicle Assembly Building." loading="eager" src="1-artemis29.jpg"/><figcaption>Artemis III comes together at Kennedy. Core-stage stacking is today’s hardware milestone (story 14), ahead of integrated tests. Photograph: NASA. <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/artemis-iii-sls-core-stage-joins-boosters-at-kennedy/">Source</a></figcaption></figure><article class="story" id="story-01"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI</span><span class="story-no">01</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Particle emerges with a coding model that aims to think less expensively</h3><p class="dek">New lab Particle introduced Particle-1, a coding model post-trained from DeepSeek V4.1 Flash.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>New lab Particle introduced Particle-1, a coding model post-trained from DeepSeek V4.1 Flash.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>The claim:</strong> Particle reports fewer output tokens on four software-engineering benchmarks, with pass@1 accuracy within the base model's confidence intervals.</li><li><strong>The angle:</strong> The focus is reducing the amount of reasoning spent on a task, rather than adding another large foundation model.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> The efficiency and accuracy results are the company's own tests. An API launch is not an open-weights release.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://particle.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Particle</a> <a href="https://particle.ai/blog/introducing-particle-1" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Particle launch</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-02"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI</span><span class="story-no">02</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>DiffuSpace releases Clover, an open diffusion-language preview</h3><p class="dek">DiffuSpace, a new venture from the HKU NLP Dream team, released a 35-billion-parameter diffusion model with 3 billion active parameters.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>DiffuSpace, a new venture from the HKU NLP Dream team, released a 35-billion-parameter diffusion model with 3 billion active parameters.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Available:</strong> Clover-35B-A3B-Preview has downloadable weights under Apache 2.0.</li><li><strong>The approach:</strong> Diffusion generates and refines text in parallel rather than relying only on a left-to-right token sequence.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> This is a preview; the performance comparisons come from the developer. The decision-model derivative discussed in the post is not released.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://diffuspace.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">DiffuSpace</a> <a href="https://diffuspace.ai/blog/clover-35b/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">DiffuSpace launch</a> <a href="https://huggingface.co/Diffuspace/Clover-35B-A3B-Preview" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Hugging Face model card</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-03"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI</span><span class="story-no">03</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Rime Prism puts a text checkpoint inside speech-to-speech</h3><p class="dek">Rime launched Prism, which reasons over audio but exposes its intended words before it speaks.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Rime launched Prism, which reasons over audio but exposes its intended words before it speaks.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Control:</strong> Applications can check or rewrite the text checkpoint before audio reaches a caller, while the model retains the caller's tone as input.</li><li><strong>Deployment:</strong> Rime offers cloud, private-cloud and on-premises deployment; it is building self-serve fine-tuning APIs with beta customers.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Latency, benchmark and customer comparisons are vendor measurements. The launch post reports 0.57 seconds to first audio on its benchmark, distinct from its separate roughly 300ms response claim.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.rime.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime</a> <a href="https://www.rime.ai/resources/prism-first-speech-to-speech-for-enterprise" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime launch</a> <a href="https://www.rime.ai/prism" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime Prism</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-04"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI</span><span class="story-no">04</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>SenseNova Code2Video makes motion graphics through code</h3><p class="dek">SenseTime announced SenseNova 6.8 Code2Video, a 27-billion-parameter model fine-tuned from Qwen3.8.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>SenseTime announced SenseNova 6.8 Code2Video, a 27-billion-parameter model fine-tuned from Qwen3.8.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Available:</strong> Weights and tooling are published under Apache 2.0, with a stated 512K context window.</li><li><strong>What it does:</strong> The workflow writes and revises code to produce designed video and motion graphics. It is not a photorealistic text-to-video engine.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> The release does not provide a systematic benchmark. Long agent workflows can consume substantial time and compute.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://huggingface.co/sensenova/SenseNova-6.8-Code2Video" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SenseNova model card</a> <a href="https://github.com/OpenSenseNova/SenseNova-6.8-Code2Video" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">OpenSenseNova</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-05"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI tools / open source</span><span class="story-no">05</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>GitHub Agentic Workflows reaches general availability</h3><p class="dek">GitHub made its Markdown-to-Actions agent workflows generally available, a distinct milestone from this week's credential fixes.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>GitHub made its Markdown-to-Actions agent workflows generally available, a distinct milestone from this week's credential fixes.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>How it works:</strong> Authors write natural-language workflow instructions that compile into GitHub Actions configuration.</li><li><strong>Boundaries:</strong> Read-only permissions are the default; safe outputs and threat checks constrain write actions. New features include budget forecasting and model routing.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> The default Copilot engine needs a qualifying Copilot license. General availability does not remove the need to review permissions.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-28-github-agentic-workflows-generally-available/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Changelog</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-06"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI tools / open source</span><span class="story-no">06</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Copilot Projects brings shared context and desktop actions</h3><p class="dek">GitHub introduced Copilot Projects and a computer-use public preview for its desktop app.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>GitHub introduced Copilot Projects and a computer-use public preview for its desktop app.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Projects:</strong> Shared project context lets agents work across repositories and related tasks.</li><li><strong>Computer use:</strong> The preview can interact with macOS and Windows applications, with approval prompts and organization-level controls.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> This is preview access, not a promise of unrestricted or unattended desktop automation.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-28-github-copilot-app-universe-releases/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Changelog</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-07"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI tools / open source</span><span class="story-no">07</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Google previews Connected Apps across Search and Gemini</h3><p class="dek">Google opened a phased developer preview for apps that connect to its AI products.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Google opened a phased developer preview for apps that connect to its AI products.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Interfaces:</strong> Connected Apps uses cloud MCP servers and Android AppFunctions, with a console to build, test and submit integrations.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> The proposed reach spans Search and Gemini, rather than requiring a separate assistant destination for each service.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Access is phased and preview-stage. This is not general availability in every market.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/connected-apps-one-place/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google</a> <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020644/google-will-let-more-developers-make-apps-that-plug-into-search-and-gemini" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">The Verge</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-08"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI tools / open source</span><span class="story-no">08</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>FlashMap drafts a block of tokens in one pass</h3><p class="dek">Researchers released FlashMap, a flow-map drafter for speculative decoding that models dependencies within a proposed token block.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Researchers released FlashMap, a flow-map drafter for speculative decoding that models dependencies within a proposed token block.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Mechanism:</strong> A target model verifies the proposed block, preserving its output distribution rather than accepting the drafter's guesses unchecked.</li><li><strong>Evidence:</strong> The paper reports up to 6.14x acceleration on a Qwen3-8B math task and 7.4% average improvement over a matched DFlash baseline.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Tests cover two sizes from one model family, at concurrency one. Batched production serving was not evaluated.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://jshen.net/flashmap/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">FlashMap project</a> <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.24365" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">FlashMap paper</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-09"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">AI tools / open source</span><span class="story-no">09</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>nanobot 0.4 adds durable conversation state</h3><p class="dek">The nanobot project released version 0.4.0 with SQLite-backed history and a revised browser workbench.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>The nanobot project released version 0.4.0 with SQLite-backed history and a revised browser workbench.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Continuity:</strong> The gateway stores model history and browser transcripts, and imports existing conversations.</li><li><strong>Controls:</strong> Stable and opt-in preview releases share an update flow with confirmation before installation and restart.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Upgrade and recovery behavior depends on how the installation is managed. Recovery does not mean restoring an old copy of chats.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://github.com/HKUDS/nanobot/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">nanobot releases</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-10"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Tech</span><span class="story-no">10</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Supabase opens OrioleDB beta for update-heavy Postgres</h3><p class="dek">Supabase made the OrioleDB storage engine available in public beta on every plan.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Supabase made the OrioleDB storage engine available in public beta on every plan.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>The design:</strong> Undo logs and index-organized tables aim to reduce dead-tuple cleanup and index churn in update-heavy workloads.</li><li><strong>Availability:</strong> Choose it when creating a new project; it cannot be added to an existing project or removed later.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Not production-ready and no SLA. Point-in-Time Recovery and restore-to-new-project are unavailable; some index types use an experimental bridge.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://supabase.com/blog/orioledb-public-beta" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Supabase</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-11"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Tech</span><span class="story-no">11</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Altera announces Agilex 2X, with silicon still ahead</h3><p class="dek">Altera outlined four FPGA families under Agilex 2X, starting with Agilex 27.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Altera outlined four FPGA families under Agilex 2X, starting with Agilex 27.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Roadmap:</strong> Early-access software is due in mid-November; engineering silicon for Agilex 27 is planned for the second half of 2027.</li><li><strong>Available now:</strong> Quartus IQ 26.3 software is available, separately from the future chips.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> This is a product roadmap, not shipping production silicon. Performance and efficiency claims are Altera's projections.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.altera.com/newsroom/agilex-2x" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Altera</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-12"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">12</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>CISA adds exploited VMware ESX and Linux flaws</h3><p class="dek">CISA added two vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on October 28.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>CISA added two vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on October 28.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>The entries:</strong> CVE-2026-41709 affects VMware ESX and concerns insufficient logging. CVE-2026-46300 concerns missing initialization in Linux.</li><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> A KEV listing signals exploitation evidence, moving these beyond hypothetical vulnerabilities.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Consult the linked catalog and vendor guidance for affected systems and remediation. This briefing does not verify a specific patch version.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2026/10/28/cisa-adds-two-known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CISA</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-13"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">13</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Node2Shell moves from disclosure to exploitation reports</h3><p class="dek">New reporting adds CISA listing and honeypot evidence to the previously covered Brotli disclosure.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>New reporting adds CISA listing and honeypot evidence to the previously covered Brotli disclosure.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Fresh development:</strong> CISA added CVE-2026-111497 on October 27; SecurityWeek reported two firms' honeypot observations on October 28.</li><li><strong>What was observed:</strong> Researchers recorded exploitation attempts. One operator said a captured exploit's final stage was broken.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> An attack attempt is not proof of a successful compromise. This is an exploitation-status update, not a new flaw announcement.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.securityweek.com/cisa-warns-of-node2shell-exploitation-as-honeypots-log-attack-attempts/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SecurityWeek</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-14"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">14</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Artemis III hardware comes together at Kennedy</h3><p class="dek">NASA joined the Artemis III SLS core stage to its solid rocket boosters at Kennedy Space Center.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>NASA joined the Artemis III SLS core stage to its solid rocket boosters at Kennedy Space Center.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Milestone:</strong> The assembly step moves the launcher toward integrated checks and later tanking tests.</li><li><strong>Mission context:</strong> Artemis III is planned as an Earth-orbit rendezvous and docking mission, not the crewed lunar landing.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> A stacking milestone is not launch readiness. More testing and integration remain.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/artemis-iii-sls-core-stage-joins-boosters-at-kennedy/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">NASA</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-15"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">15</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>NASA and Google test a robot that fetches medical gear</h3><p class="dek">An October 27 Google report describes a NASA proof of concept for a supervised logistics and medical-support robot.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>An October 27 Google report describes a NASA proof of concept for a supervised logistics and medical-support robot.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Lab result:</strong> A Pi0-based model achieved an initial 85% success rate locating and moving a medical bag in early lab tests.</li><li><strong>Autonomy:</strong> The team tested local operation on compact edge hardware, relevant where Earth communications are delayed.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> This is a supervised proof of concept, not an autonomous physician or a system deployed on a spacecraft.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-27</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/public-sector/google-cloud-and-nasa-testing-robotic-systems-for-deep-space-missions" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google Cloud</a> <a href="https://www.nextgov.com/emerging-tech/2026/10/nasa-google-test-ai-powered-medical-robot-deep-space-missions/416874/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Nextgov/FCW</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-16"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">16</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Vulcan targets a November 13 return</h3><p class="dek">ULA is targeting November 13 for Vulcan's return to flight, carrying 40 Amazon Leo satellites.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>ULA is targeting November 13 for Vulcan's return to flight, carrying 40 Amazon Leo satellites.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Changes:</strong> The mission uses six boosters and a revised nozzle design after the earlier anomaly.</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> Ars Technica reports the target remains subject to range approval.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> A target date is not a confirmed launch. The root cause has not been publicly detailed.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/10/ulas-vulcan-rocket-is-set-to-return-to-flight-on-friday-the-13th/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ars Technica</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-17"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Cyberpunk-space</span><span class="story-no">17</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>ESA orders four more Galileo second-generation satellites</h3><p class="dek">ESA signed for four additional Galileo G2 satellites, bringing the planned second-generation fleet to 16.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>ESA signed for four additional Galileo G2 satellites, bringing the planned second-generation fleet to 16.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Contracts:</strong> Airbus and Thales Alenia Space will each supply two additional satellites.</li><li><strong>Schedule:</strong> These four are due for delivery from 2031; the first G2 launch is planned for 2028.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Procurement expands the future constellation. It does not add operational navigation capacity today.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-27</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.esa.int/Applications/Satellite_navigation/Galileo/ESA_procures_four_new_Galileo_Second_Generation_satellites" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ESA</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-18"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Business</span><span class="story-no">18</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Microsoft reports $92.3 billion in quarterly revenue</h3><p class="dek">Microsoft reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $92.3 billion, up 19% year on year.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Microsoft reported fiscal 2027 first-quarter revenue of $92.3 billion, up 19% year on year.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Cloud:</strong> Microsoft Cloud revenue reached $63.9 billion, up 30%; Azure and other cloud services grew 48%.</li><li><strong>Period:</strong> These results cover the quarter ended September 30, 2026, not a calendar-year total.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Revenue growth is not a measure of customer return on AI investment. The company's release is the source of these figures.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://news.microsoft.com/source/2026/10/28/microsoft-announces-first-quarter-results-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Microsoft</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-19"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Business</span><span class="story-no">19</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Alphabet's cloud growth meets a cash-hungry buildout</h3><p class="dek">Alphabet reported third-quarter revenue of $127.6 billion, up 25%, with Google Cloud revenue more than doubling.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Alphabet reported third-quarter revenue of $127.6 billion, up 25%, with Google Cloud revenue more than doubling.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Cloud:</strong> Google Cloud revenue rose 103% to $30.7 billion, including a meaningful contribution from TPU systems sales.</li><li><strong>Cash:</strong> The earnings filing reports negative $9.07 billion of quarterly free cash flow as investment spending continues.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Cloud revenue includes infrastructure and other services. It is not a clean measure of standalone AI-model revenue.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1652044/000165204426000080/googexhibit991q32026.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Alphabet SEC earnings release</a> <a href="https://blog.google/company-news/inside-google/message-ceo/alphabet-earnings-q3-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google CEO remarks</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-20"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Business</span><span class="story-no">20</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Foundry Robotics raises $100 million for assembly as a service</h3><p class="dek">Foundry Robotics announced a $100 million Series A led by Marlinspike Partners.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Foundry Robotics announced a $100 million Series A led by Marlinspike Partners.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Model:</strong> The company plans to operate its own assembly capacity as manufacturing-as-a-service, rather than only sell robots.</li><li><strong>Facility:</strong> Its 130,000-square-foot Emeryville factory targets industrial and defense customers with robot workcells and human operations.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> The funding is raised; fully autonomous production at scale is the company's ambition, not an independently established result.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://foundryrobotics.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Foundry Robotics</a> <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20261028508802/en/Foundry-Robotics-Raises-%24100-Million-Series-A-to-Build-Americas-First-Autonomous-Factory-for-Assembly" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Foundry Robotics / Business Wire</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-21"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Business</span><span class="story-no">21</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Upwind raises $400 million for cloud and AI security</h3><p class="dek">Upwind announced a $400 million Series C led by Bessemer Venture Partners.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Upwind announced a $400 million Series C led by Bessemer Venture Partners.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Capital:</strong> The company says total funding now exceeds $800 million.</li><li><strong>Direction:</strong> It is expanding its cloud and AI security platform and launching an AI cybersecurity research lab.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> The funding does not independently validate the company's product performance or security claims.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.upwind.io/feed/the-next-wave-is-here-upwinds-400m-series-c-to-build-the-cloud-ai-security-platform" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Upwind</a></div></div></details></article><article class="story" id="story-22"><div class="story-meta"><span class="tag">Business</span><span class="story-no">22</span><span class="read-time">1 min read</span></div><h3>Global bond sales hit $6.61 trillion amid AI borrowing</h3><p class="dek">Bloomberg reports global bond issuance reached a record $6.61 trillion, about 15% above the comparable year-earlier pace.</p><details><summary>Read the briefing <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="reading"><p class="brief-lead"><strong>Bloomberg reports global bond issuance reached a record $6.61 trillion, about 15% above the comparable year-earlier pace.</strong></p><ul class="brief"><li><strong>Scope:</strong> The total includes governments, financial institutions and companies, across investment-grade and high-yield markets.</li><li><strong>AI connection:</strong> Technology's infrastructure borrowing is a contributor to the record, not the whole total.</li><li><strong>Caveat:</strong> Only the readable article lead and takeaways were used. Do not read the $6.61 trillion as an AI-only debt figure.</li></ul><p class="source-label">2026-10-28</p><div class="source-links"><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-28/global-bond-sales-shatter-record-fueled-by-ai-borrowing-binge" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Bloomberg</a></div></div></details></article></section><section class="colophon" id="sources"><h2>Source index</h2><ol class="source-index"><li value="1">Particle emerges with a coding model that aims to think less expensively: <a href="https://particle.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Particle</a> <a href="https://particle.ai/blog/introducing-particle-1" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Particle launch</a> </li><li value="2">DiffuSpace releases Clover, an open diffusion-language preview: <a href="https://diffuspace.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">DiffuSpace</a> <a href="https://diffuspace.ai/blog/clover-35b/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">DiffuSpace launch</a> <a href="https://huggingface.co/Diffuspace/Clover-35B-A3B-Preview" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Hugging Face model card</a> </li><li value="3">Rime Prism puts a text checkpoint inside speech-to-speech: <a href="https://www.rime.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime</a> <a href="https://www.rime.ai/resources/prism-first-speech-to-speech-for-enterprise" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime launch</a> <a href="https://www.rime.ai/prism" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Rime Prism</a> </li><li value="4">SenseNova Code2Video makes motion graphics through code: <a href="https://huggingface.co/sensenova/SenseNova-6.8-Code2Video" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SenseNova model card</a> <a href="https://github.com/OpenSenseNova/SenseNova-6.8-Code2Video" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">OpenSenseNova</a> </li><li value="5">GitHub Agentic Workflows reaches general availability: <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-28-github-agentic-workflows-generally-available/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Changelog</a> </li><li value="6">Copilot Projects brings shared context and desktop actions: <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-10-28-github-copilot-app-universe-releases/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub Changelog</a> </li><li value="7">Google previews Connected Apps across Search and Gemini: <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/connected-apps-one-place/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google</a> <a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1020644/google-will-let-more-developers-make-apps-that-plug-into-search-and-gemini" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">The Verge</a> </li><li value="8">FlashMap drafts a block of tokens in one pass: <a href="https://jshen.net/flashmap/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">FlashMap project</a> <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2610.24365" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">FlashMap paper</a> </li><li value="9">nanobot 0.4 adds durable conversation state: <a href="https://github.com/HKUDS/nanobot/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">nanobot releases</a> </li><li value="10">Supabase opens OrioleDB beta for update-heavy Postgres: <a href="https://supabase.com/blog/orioledb-public-beta" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Supabase</a> </li><li value="11">Altera announces Agilex 2X, with silicon still ahead: <a href="https://www.altera.com/newsroom/agilex-2x" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Altera</a> </li><li value="12">CISA adds exploited VMware ESX and Linux flaws: <a href="https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/alerts/2026/10/28/cisa-adds-two-known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CISA</a> </li><li value="13">Node2Shell moves from disclosure to exploitation reports: <a href="https://www.securityweek.com/cisa-warns-of-node2shell-exploitation-as-honeypots-log-attack-attempts/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">SecurityWeek</a> </li><li value="14">Artemis III hardware comes together at Kennedy: <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/artemis-iii-sls-core-stage-joins-boosters-at-kennedy/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">NASA</a> </li><li value="15">NASA and Google test a robot that fetches medical gear: <a href="https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/public-sector/google-cloud-and-nasa-testing-robotic-systems-for-deep-space-missions" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google Cloud</a> <a href="https://www.nextgov.com/emerging-tech/2026/10/nasa-google-test-ai-powered-medical-robot-deep-space-missions/416874/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Nextgov/FCW</a> </li><li value="16">Vulcan targets a November 13 return: <a href="https://arstechnica.com/space/2026/10/ulas-vulcan-rocket-is-set-to-return-to-flight-on-friday-the-13th/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Ars Technica</a> </li><li value="17">ESA orders four more Galileo second-generation satellites: <a href="https://www.esa.int/Applications/Satellite_navigation/Galileo/ESA_procures_four_new_Galileo_Second_Generation_satellites" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ESA</a> </li><li value="18">Microsoft reports $92.3 billion in quarterly revenue: <a href="https://news.microsoft.com/source/2026/10/28/microsoft-announces-first-quarter-results-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Microsoft</a> </li><li value="19">Alphabet's cloud growth meets a cash-hungry buildout: <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1652044/000165204426000080/googexhibit991q32026.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Alphabet SEC earnings release</a> <a href="https://blog.google/company-news/inside-google/message-ceo/alphabet-earnings-q3-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Google CEO remarks</a> </li><li value="20">Foundry Robotics raises $100 million for assembly as a service: <a href="https://foundryrobotics.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Foundry Robotics</a> <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20261028508802/en/Foundry-Robotics-Raises-%24100-Million-Series-A-to-Build-Americas-First-Autonomous-Factory-for-Assembly" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Foundry Robotics / Business Wire</a> </li><li value="21">Upwind raises $400 million for cloud and AI security: <a href="https://www.upwind.io/feed/the-next-wave-is-here-upwinds-400m-series-c-to-build-the-cloud-ai-security-platform" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Upwind</a> </li><li value="22">Global bond sales hit $6.61 trillion amid AI borrowing: <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-28/global-bond-sales-shatter-record-fueled-by-ai-borrowing-binge" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Bloomberg</a> </li></ol><p class="caption">Intro/outro music: "Muted Trumpet Coffee Shop Warm Quick Set" by Alex Morgan via Free Music Archive, CC BY 4.0, trimmed. <a href="https://freemusicarchive.org/music/alex-morgan/cocktail-bar-lounge-jazz-vol-3/muted-trumpet-coffee-shop-warm-quick-set/">Track and license</a>.</p></section></main><script>(() => {
  'use strict';
  const root = document.documentElement;
  const toggle = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
  const themeUI = () => {
    const dark = root.dataset.theme === 'dark';
    toggle.innerHTML = `${dark ? 'Light' : 'Dark'} mode <span aria-hidden="true">${dark ? '☀' : '◐'}</span>`;
    toggle.setAttribute('aria-label', `Switch to ${dark ? 'light' : 'dark'} theme`);
    toggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(dark));
  };
  themeUI();
  toggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
    root.dataset.theme = root.dataset.theme === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
    try { localStorage.setItem('edit-theme', root.dataset.theme); } catch (_) {}
    themeUI();
  });
  const format = seconds => Number.isFinite(seconds) ? `${Math.floor(seconds / 60)}:${String(Math.floor(seconds % 60)).padStart(2, '0')}` : '0:00';
  document.querySelectorAll('.audio-player').forEach(player => {
    const audio = document.getElementById(player.dataset.audio);
    const button = player.querySelector('.audio-play');
    const seek = player.querySelector('.audio-seek');
    const error = player.nextElementSibling;
    const title = player.dataset.title;
    const status = player.querySelector('.audio-status');
    const update = () => {
      player.querySelector('.elapsed').textContent = format(audio.currentTime);
      if (Number.isFinite(audio.duration)) {
        player.querySelector('.duration').textContent = format(Math.round(audio.duration));
        seek.max = audio.duration;
        seek.value = audio.currentTime;
        seek.style.setProperty('--progress', `${audio.currentTime / audio.duration * 100}%`);
      }
      seek.setAttribute('aria-valuetext', `${format(audio.currentTime)} of ${player.querySelector('.duration').textContent}`);
      button.setAttribute('aria-label', `${audio.paused ? 'Play' : 'Pause'} ${title}`);
      player.querySelector('.play-symbol').textContent = audio.paused ? '▶' : 'Ⅱ';
    };
    button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      if (!audio.paused) { audio.pause(); return; }
      document.querySelectorAll('audio').forEach(other => { if (other !== audio) other.pause(); });
      error.hidden = true;
      status.textContent = 'Loading audio…';
      try { await audio.play(); } catch (_) {
        status.textContent = 'Playback unavailable';
        error.textContent = 'Audio could not start. Try play again or download the recording below.';
        error.hidden = false;
        update();
      }
    });
    seek.addEventListener('input', () => {
      if (Number.isFinite(audio.duration)) { audio.currentTime = Number(seek.value); update(); }
    });
    ['loadedmetadata', 'durationchange', 'timeupdate', 'seeked'].forEach(event => audio.addEventListener(event, update));
    audio.addEventListener('playing', () => { status.textContent = 'Now playing'; error.hidden = true; update(); });
    audio.addEventListener('pause', () => { status.textContent = 'Paused'; update(); });
    audio.addEventListener('waiting', () => { status.textContent = 'Loading audio…'; });
    audio.addEventListener('ended', () => { status.textContent = 'Finished'; update(); });
    audio.addEventListener('error', () => { status.textContent = 'Audio unavailable'; error.textContent = 'Audio could not load. Try the download link below.'; error.hidden = false; });
    update();
  });
})();
</script></body></html>
